            <div xml:id="d72">

                <pb facs="0083" n="79" sameAs="frus:frus1939v03/pg79"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.9315 Manchuria/194: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-11-13T19:00:00+03:00">November 13,
                            1939—7 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 13—2:26 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>890. Although not reported in the Soviet press, information indicates, I
                    understand, that the discussions which have been taking place here between the
                    Japanese Ambassador and Molotov in regard to the settlement of boundary
                    questions has resulted in an agreement for the delimitation of the Mongolian and
                    Manchurian border and the appointment of a commission composed of 20 Soviet
                    representatives and 20 Japanese to meet in the very near future at Chita for
                    this purpose. 1 further understand that when the Mongolian-Manchurian border
                    question has been settled the commission will proceed to Harbin to take up the
                    establishment of the Soviet-Manchurian frontier.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
